    {
      "id": "cgas_sting_ifnb",
      "title": "cGAS-STING → IFN-β",
      "pathway": "Cytosolic DNA Sensing → IRF3 / NF-κB → IFN-β",
      "renderer": "builtin",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "roundsToUnlock": 3,
      "slotsPerRound": 2,
      "description": "Cytosolic double-stranded DNA — from viruses, intracellular bacteria, or damaged host cells — is detected by cGAS (cyclic GMP-AMP synthase). cGAS catalyzes synthesis of the second messenger 2'3'-cGAMP, which binds and activates STING (Stimulator of Interferon Genes) at the endoplasmic reticulum membrane.",
      "teachingPoint": "Upon cGAMP binding, STING dimerizes and translocates from the ER to the Golgi, where it recruits TBK1. TBK1 phosphorylates IRF3 to drive IFN-β production. STING simultaneously activates NF-κB via IKKβ, producing pro-inflammatory cytokines. This pathway defends against DNA viruses (HSV-1, vaccinia) and intracellular bacteria, and causes autoimmunity when cytosolic self-DNA accumulates (e.g., STING-associated vasculopathy, lupus).",
