    {
      "id": "tlr4_myd88_nfkb",
      "title": "TLR4-MyD88 → NF-κB",
      "pathway": "LPS Recognition → NF-κB → Pro-inflammatory Cytokines",
      "renderer": "builtin",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "roundsToUnlock": 4,
      "slotsPerRound": 2,
      "description": "Lipopolysaccharide (LPS), a structural component of the Gram-negative bacterial outer membrane, is recognized at the cell surface by TLR4 in complex with MD-2. LPS binding drives TLR4 dimerization and cytoplasmic recruitment of the adaptor MyD88 via TIR domain–TIR domain interactions.",
      "teachingPoint": "MyD88 recruits IRAK4, which phosphorylates IRAK1, activating TRAF6. TRAF6 generates K63-linked ubiquitin chains that activate IKKβ. IKKβ phosphorylates IκBα (marking it for proteasomal degradation), releasing NF-κB (p50/RelA) to drive transcription of TNF-α, IL-6, and IL-12. Note: IKKβ inhibits IκBα, and IκBα normally inhibits NF-κB — so the net effect of IKKβ is NF-κB activation.",
      "nucleus": {
        "cx": 50,
        "cy": 85,
        "rx": 46,
        "ry": 13
      },
